    "lawCode": "Amd §3006, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5742\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the tax law, in relation to representatives holding\npower of attorney\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nEnsures that when taxpayers are represented by an Enrolled Agent on\nState tax matters, the NYS Department of Taxation and Finance will\nonly communicate with the taxpayer through the taxpayer's Enrolled\nAgent.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Amends subdivision (c) of section 3006 of the Tax Law to\nadd new language forbidding the Department of Taxation and Finance\nfrom communicating with a taxpayer (by phone or in person) over the\ncollection of any unpaid tax if the Department knows that the taxpayer\nis represented by a person authorized to practice before the US\nInternal Revenue Service holding a valid power of attorney.\n\nSection 2: Establishes the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nAn Enrolled Agent is an authorized tax preparer licensed to practice\nand represent clients before the federal Internal Revenue Service\n(IRS). They also represent New York State taxpayers on state tax\nissues. The clients of Enrolled Agents often sign \"powers of attorney\"\nauthorizing their representation and directing the IRS to send all\ncommunications through the Enrolled Agent. Federal law and regulation\nrequires the IRS to communicate exclusively through the Enrolled\nAgent, and requires the IRS to respect those \"powers of attorney\".\n\nIn New York, Enrolled Agents have had difficulty with the NYS\nDepartment of Taxation and Finance respecting \"powers of attorney\",\nand continuing to communicate directly with the represented taxpayer.\nThis leads to frustration and mistrust with both Enrolled Agents\nworking in New York and their clients. It can also delay the\nresolution of these matters.\n\nThis legislation makes it clear that if a taxpayer is represented by\nan Enrolled Agent, the Department of Taxation and Finance must only\ncommunicate with the taxpayer through their agent. This bill makes\nexceptions if the taxpayer consents to direct communication, or fails\nto respond in a timely manner with the Department.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015: A.5359-B,\n2016: A.8674\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately\n",
