 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Removes provisions relating to an officer or employee notifying the taxpayer directly that such officer or employee believes the representative holding power of attorney is responsible for unreasonable delay or hindrance of a division of taxation examination or investigation of the taxpayer.",
 "title": "Relates to representatives holding power of attorney",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "S5742",
     "basePrintNoStr": "S5742-2017",
     "printNo": "S5742",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 232\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 5742, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend the tax law, in relation to representatives holding\n      power of attorney\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  amend  the  Tax Law to prohibit contact between the\nDepartment of Taxation and Finance and taxpayers when  the  taxpayer  is\nrepresented by an Internal Revenue Service (IRS) enrolled agent or other\nrepresentative.  Direct  contact  may  only  occur  when  the Department\nreceives prior consent from the taxpayer, his or her enrolled agent,  or\notherwise is permitted by court order.\n \n  Current  law already requires the Department to recognize IRS enrolled\nagents as representatives of taxpayers. In 2015, I vetoed a similar bill\nthat would have prohibited contact between the Department and  taxpayers\nrepresented by an enrolled agent. (Veto No. 231 of 2015).\n \n  This   bill  would  continue  to  impose  additional  and  unnecessary\nrestrictions on the Department that would negatively impact the  taxpay-\ners.  First,  the  requirement  of  written-only communications with the\ntaxpayer's representatives would potentially delay resolution  of  time-\nsensitive matters, such as imminent seizures or levies. Second, the bill\nwould  eliminate statutory language authorizing the Department to notify\nthe taxpayer directly if the Department believes the  representative  is\nresponsible for unreasonable delay or hindering an examination or inves-\ntigation.  Third,  taxpayers  often  have multiple powers of attorney on\nfile as they will appoint different representatives to handle  different\ntax matters. This bill would require the consent of every representative\nbefore  the  Department  could  initiate  direct  communication with the\ntaxpayer, regardless of the nature of the inquiry. For these reasons,  I\nam constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  Given there are exigent circumstances where the Department may need to\ncommunicate with the taxpayer directly, I am directing the Department to\nreview  its existing policies and procedures to ensure it has the neces-\nsary safeguards in place to prevent  employees  from  initiating  direct\ncommunication  with  a  taxpayer prior to confirming there is a power of\nattorney form on file.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
